<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  A deliberately near-empty Info.plist.

	  The target still has GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES and keeps declaring the scene
	  manifest, orientations and launch screen through INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings;
	  Xcode merges those into this file rather than replacing it. This exists only for
	  the one key that has no INFOPLIST_KEY_ equivalent — the build system silently
	  drops INFOPLIST_KEY_UIBackgroundModes, since background modes are normally
	  written here by the Signing & Capabilities editor.

	  remote-notification is what lets CloudKit wake the app on a silent push and pull
	  changes down in the background. Without it sync still works, but only while the
	  app is in the foreground.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  A party is the phones in one car talking to each other directly, so iOS asks
	  for local network access the first time one is started or looked for.

	  Both _tcp and _udp are required. MultipeerConnectivity uses both, and
	  declaring only the tcp half is the classic way to end up with a browser that
	  runs happily and simply never finds anybody — no error, no prompt, nothing.
	  The service name must match `PartySession.service` exactly.
	-->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Plates uses the local network to find the other phones in your car, so everyone can share the plates they spot.</string>
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_plates-party._tcp</string>
		<string>_plates-party._udp</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Required before iOS will offer this app as the thing that opens a CloudKit
	  share link. Without it the recipient taps the invitation and is told they need
	  a newer version of the app — which is not a version problem at all, and gives
	  no hint that a missing plist key is the cause. Sharing a book cannot work
	  without this, and nothing in the code can compensate for its absence.
	-->
	<key>CKSharingSupported</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Voice mode listens while you drive so you can call out plates instead of tapping them.</string>
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Voice mode turns what you say into plate sightings. Recognition runs on your iPhone wherever the device supports it, so your speech does not leave it.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
